e us.' They came back and whispered to him: 'Now do be serious, and hand us out something.' Then Thumbling again cried as loudly as he could: 'I really will give you everything, only put your hands in.' The maid who was listening heard this quite distinctly, jumped out of bed and rushed to the door. The thieves took flight and ran as if the wild huntsman were behind them, but the maid could see nothing, and went to light a candle. When she got to the place with it, Thumbling, unperceived, betook himself to the granary, and the maid, after she had examined every corner and found nothing, lay down in her bed again, and believed that she had been dreaming with open eyes and ears. Thumbling, however, had crept amongst the hay and found a beautiful place to sleep in: there he intended to rest till day, and then go home again to his parents. But he had other things to go through first. There is, indeed, a great deal to go through in this world! When day dawned, and the maid got up to feed the cows, the first thing she did was to go into the granary and fetch an armful of hay, and it happened to be the very armful in which Thumbling was lying asleep. He, however, slept so soundly that he was aware of nothing, and did not awake until he was in the mouth of the cow, which had picked him up with the hay. 'Ah, heavens!' cried he, 'how did I get into the mill?' but he soon found out where he was. It was necessary to be careful not to get between the teeth, or he would have been crushed. In this strait he had to slip down the throat. 'The windows of this room have been forgotten,' said he, 'and no sun shines into it, neither is a candle brought.' On the whole his quarters were especially unpleasing to him, and the worst was, more and more hay was always coming in by the door, and the space grew less and less. At last in his anguish, he cried as loud as he could: 'Bring me no more fodder, bring me no more fodder!' The maid was just milking the cow, and when she heard someone spea