daughter shuddered, but the King said: 'I have sworn to give you to the very first beggar, and I will keep my word.' All pleading was in vain. The priest was brought, and she had to let herself be wedded to the fiddler on the spot. When that was done the King said: 'Now it is not proper for you, a beggar's wife, to stay any longer in my palace, you may just go away with your husband.' The beggar-man led her out by the hand, and she was obliged to walk away on foot with him. When they came to a large forest she asked: 'To whom does that beautiful forest belong?' 'It belongs to King Thrushbeard; if you had taken him, it would all have been yours.' 'Ah, unhappy girl that I am, if I had but taken King Thrushbeard!' Next they came to a meadow. 'To whom does this beautiful green meadow belong?' 'It belongs to King Thrushbeard; if you had taken him, it would all have been yours.' 'Ah, unhappy girl that I am, if I had but taken King Thrushbeard!' Then they came to a large town. 'To whom does this fine large town belong?' 'It belongs to King Thrushbeard; if you had taken him, it would all have been yours.' 'Ah, unhappy girl that I am, if I had but taken King Thrushbeard!' 'It does not please me,' said the fiddler, 'to hear you always wishing for another husband; am I not good enough for you?' At last they came to a very little hut, and she said: 'Oh, good heavens! what a small house! To whom does this miserable, tiny hut belong?' The fiddler answered: 'That is my house and yours, where we shall live together.' She had to stoop to get in at the low door. 'Where are the servants?' said the King's daughter. 'What servants?' answered the beggar-man; 'you must yourself do what you wish to have done. Just make a fire at once, and put on water to cook my supper, I am quite tired.' The King's daughter knew nothing about lighting fires or cooking, and the beggar-man had to lend a hand himself to get anything fairly done. When they had finished their scanty meal they went to bed; but the fiddler called her up quite early in the morning to clean the house. For a few days they lived in this way as well as might be, and then came to the end of their provisions. Then the man said: 'Wife, we cannot go on any longer eating and drinking here and earning nothing. You must weave baskets.' He went out, cut some willows, and brought them home. Then she began to weave, but the tough willows wounded her delicate hands. 'I see that this won't do,' said the man; 'try spinning, perhaps you will do that better.' She sat down and tried to spin, but the hard thread soon cut her soft fingers till the blood ran down. 'You see,' said the man, 'you are fit for no sort of work; I have made a bad bargain with you. Now I will try to make a business with pots and earthenware; you must sit in the market and sell the ware.' 'Alas,' thought she, 'if any of the people from my father's kingdom come to the market and see me sitting there, selling, how they will laugh at me!' But it was of no use, she had to yield unless she chose to die of hunger. For the first time she behaved herself well, for she was fair, and the people bought her wares willingly, and paid her what she asked; many of them even gave her the money and left the pots with her as well. So they lived on what she had earned as long as it lasted, then the husband bought a great many new pots. With these she seated herself in a corner of the market-place, and set them out in front of her for sale. But a drunken hussar came by, and rode his horse against her pots so that they were all broken into a thousand bits. She began to cry, and did not know what to do for fear. 'Ah, what will become of me!' said she; 'what will my husband say to this!' She ran home and told him of the misfortune. 'Who would have thought you would have been so silly as to put an earthenware stall in the corner of the market-place, where every one knocks up against it!' said he; 'but let us leave off crying. I see you are not fit for any ordinary work, so I have been to our King's palace, and have asked whether they cannot find a place for a kitchen-maid, and they have promised me to take you; in this way you will get your food for nothing.' The King's daughter was now a kitchen-maid, and had to be at the cook's beck and call, and do the dirtiest work. In both her pockets she fastened a little jar, in which she took home what was left of what was served at table, and upon this they lived. It happened that the wedding of the King's eldest son was to be celebrated, and the poor woman went up and placed herself by the door of the hall to look on. When all the candles were lit, and people, each more beautiful than the other, entered, and all was full of pomp and splendour, she thought of her lot with a sad heart, and cursed the pride and haughtiness which had humbled her and brought her so low. The smell of the delicious dishes which were being taken in and out reached her, and the servants threw her now and then a bit of them: that she put in her jars to take home. All at once the King's son entered, clothed in velvet and silk. He saw the beautiful woman standing by the door, took her by the hand, and wanted to dance with her, but she refused and shrank with fear, for she saw that it was King Thrushbeard, who was courting her, and whom she had mocked. Her struggles were of no avail; he drew her into the hall. Then the string by which her pockets were hung broke, the pots fell down, the soup ran out, and the scraps were scattered all about. When the people saw it, there was general laughter and derision, and she was so ashamed that she would have liked to have been a thousand fathoms under the ground. She sprang to the door and wanted to run away, but a man caught her and brought her back; and when she looked at him it was King Thrushbeard again. He said to her kindly: 'Be not afraid, I and the fiddler who lived with you in the wretched hut are one. For love of you I disguised myself so; and I also was the hussar who rode his horse against your pots. This was all done to humble your proud spirit, and to punish you for the insolence with which you mocked me.' Then she wept bitterly and said: 'I have done great wrong, and am not worthy to be your wife.' But he said: 'Be comforted, the evil days are past; now we will celebrate our wedding.' Then the maids-in-waiting came and put on her the most splendid clothing, and her father and his whole court were there already to wish her happiness on her marriage with King Thrushbeard, and the joy now began in earnest. I wish you and I had been there too."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()
